PRE-REGISTRATION -- is RETRIEVAL RECALL the lever?              2026-09-12
Written at 11:06, BEFORE the TOPK20 or TOPK40 arms reported. Only FULL (60.2%)
and TOPK10 (57.6% at recall 67.6%) are known. Timestamped deliberately: this
hypothesis is motivated by TOPK10 alone, and if TOPK20 lands first it becomes
unfalsifiable as a prediction.

THE OBSERVATION IT RESTS ON
  TOPK10 scored 57.6% while holding the correct skill only 67.6% of the time.
  Conditional on the answer being present, it chose correctly 85.2% of the time.
  FULL holds the answer 100% of the time and chooses correctly 60.2%.
  So a 10-item shortlist appears to make the model ~25 points BETTER at choosing,
  and the only thing holding the arm down is that the retriever loses the answer
  a third of the time.

THE HYPOTHESIS
  H-RETRIEVAL: the binding constraint on skill selection is RETRIEVER RECALL, not
  catalogue content. Catalogue SIZE was falsified as the driver (ADR-0155 fifth
  addendum: a 38%-size catalogue scored slightly LOWER on every arm). Description
  SHORTENING was retracted (-8.8pp). Both attacked the catalogue. If H-RETRIEVAL
  holds, the catalogue was never the lever and three passes were spent on the
  wrong object.

WHAT WOULD FOLLOW, quantitatively
  The retriever is plain BM25 over name+description with a 3-char token filter --
  the weakest reasonable baseline. Its measured ceilings are recall@10 68.3%,
  @20 72.9%, @40 81.0%. If conditional accuracy stays near 85% and recall can be
  lifted to ~90% (embeddings, query expansion, or simply a better tokenizer),
  the projected arm score is ~76% against FULL's 60.2%.
  That would be the largest lever this campaign has found, and it is CHEAPER
  than FULL rather than more expensive.

PREDICTIONS, fixed now
  R1 Conditional accuracy FALLS as K grows -- more siblings in the shortlist is
     the sibling-confusion mechanism itself. I predict TOPK20's conditional
     accuracy is BELOW TOPK10's 85.2%, and TOPK40's below TOPK20's.
  R2 Despite R1, TOPK40 scores HIGHEST of the three TOPK arms, because recall
     rises faster (68.3 -> 81.0) than conditional accuracy falls.
  R3 At least one TOPK arm beats FULL's 60.2% on the point estimate.
  R4 A cheap retriever improvement (better tokenization alone -- keeping 2-char
     tokens, splitting on hyphens, stemming) lifts recall@20 by >=3 points over
     the measured 72.9%. This is the only prediction here that is testable with
     NO model calls at all, so it is the one to run first and the cheapest way
     to falsify H-RETRIEVAL early.

DECISION RULES
  R4 fails (tokenizer change moves recall@20 by <3 points) -> the retriever is
     not trivially improvable; H-RETRIEVAL survives but its CHEAP route is shut,
     and any further work needs an embedding retriever, which is a real build.
  R1 and R2 both hold AND R3 holds -> shortlisting works and recall is the knob.
     Next measurement is a better retriever at fixed K, not a different K.
  R3 fails (no TOPK arm beats FULL) -> conditional accuracy is not high enough to
     overcome the recall loss at any K tried. H-RETRIEVAL is then about a ceiling
     nobody can reach with this retriever family, and shortlisting is dead as a
     product move regardless of how attractive the conditional number looked.
     Record that the 85.2% conditional was a TRUE observation that did NOT
     translate -- that is the failure mode this pre-registration exists to catch.

KNOWN CONFOUND, stated in advance
  Conditional accuracy is computed as accuracy/recall over ARM-LEVEL aggregates,
  not per query. It assumes a query whose answer is missing from the shortlist is
  never scored correct -- true by construction, since the model can only reply
  with a name it was shown. But it also assumes the queries the retriever MISSES
  are no harder for the model than the ones it HITS, and that is NOT established:
  BM25 likely misses exactly the queries whose wording is furthest from the skill
  description, which are plausibly the hard ones. If so, conditional accuracy is
  BIASED UPWARD and the projection above is optimistic. This cannot be resolved
  from arm aggregates -- it needs per-query join of retrieval hit vs correctness,
  which choosability-topk-matrix.json supports and should be computed before any
  claim rests on the 85.2%.

=== 14:49 INSTRUMENT DEFECT FOUND AFTER THE VERDICT -- re-measurement pre-registered
Every arm above rendered 11 of 140 skills as "- name: >-": the loader read a YAML block-scalar description as its
indicator (bench/_frontmatter.py, Zak-Code #404). Those 11 scored FULL 0.364 vs 0.623 for the rest and 0.091 in every
TOPK arm (BM25 cannot retrieve a name-only entry): a drag of ~2 points on every TOPK-FULL difference, the size of the
deficits the verdict rests on. So the R3 verdict was read off a defective instrument and is VOID until re-measured.
Re-measurement: all four arms (FULL, TOPK10/20/40), same 140 skills, same queries, same model and temperature, with
the fixed loader + assert_sane. Predictions, fixed now: (a) the 11 skills' TOPK accuracy rises from 0.091 toward their
FULL level; (b) every TOPK-FULL difference moves toward zero by ~1-2 points; (c) whether TOPK20 beats FULL on the
point estimate is NOT predicted -- R3 is re-decided by the pre-registered primary (TOPK20-FULL, paired bootstrap over
skills, 95% CI) exactly as before, with the same 5-point non-inferiority bar for a cost-only reading; (d) the FULL
control moves by less than 3 points (the 11 gain a real description; nothing else changes). Fidelity with the fixed
loader, same NI-matrix accuracies: F2 rho +0.550 (was +0.637), tertile spread 41.3% -> 78.7%; recorded in ADR-0158's
second addendum with the excluding-the-11 variant beside it.

